<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Documentation Platform Migration"
  summary="Led the end-to-end migration of docs.swimlane.com from MadCap Flare to Archbee, reducing costs by ~80% while improving search, AI-assisted discovery, and content modularity."
  date="2024 - 2025"
  role="Sole Technical Writer / Migration Lead"
  tags={["Platform Migration", "Cost Optimization", "Content Architecture", "Archbee", "MadCap Flare"]}
>

<h2>Problem</h2>

<p>The documentation platform for a security automation product was running on MadCap Flare, a legacy desktop-based authoring tool. This created several compounding problems:</p>

<ul>
  <li>High annual licensing cost ($26,000+/year) with limited collaboration capabilities</li>
  <li>No native search quality or AI-assisted discovery for customers</li>
  <li>Content was tightly coupled to the tool, making modular reuse difficult</li>
  <li>Publishing workflows were manual and error-prone during product releases</li>
  <li>Support and field teams struggled to find answers quickly, increasing escalation load</li>
</ul>

<h2>My Role</h2>

<p>As the sole technical writer, I owned the entire migration end-to-end: platform evaluation, content conversion, restructuring, parallel release support during the transition, and production rollout validation.</p>

<h2>What I Did</h2>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Evaluated and selected</strong> Archbee as the target platform based on cost, API capabilities, search quality, and content modularity</li>
  <li><strong>Converted all content</strong> from MadCap Flare output to Archbee-compatible Markdown, including restructuring navigation and information architecture</li>
  <li><strong>Cleaned up legacy content</strong> by removing outdated material, consolidating duplicate topics, and improving task-based organization</li>
  <li><strong>Maintained parallel release support</strong> throughout the migration, ensuring no release shipped without documentation coverage</li>
  <li><strong>Conducted two months of platform validation</strong> before production rollout, testing search accuracy, rendering, navigation, and cross-linking</li>
  <li><strong>Coordinated production cutover</strong> with minimal disruption to customers and internal teams</li>
</ol>

<h2>Constraints</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Single-person documentation team with no migration budget for contractors</li>
  <li>Active product releases continued throughout migration (no content freeze)</li>
  <li>Legacy content had inconsistent structure, requiring manual cleanup during conversion</li>
  <li>Platform readiness had to be validated against customer-facing usage patterns, not just internal testing</li>
</ul>

<h2>Outcomes</h2>

<h3>Customer Impact</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Improved self-service with faster search and Ask AI capabilities</li>
  <li>Better content discoverability through task-based reorganization aligned to user workflows</li>
  <li>Support and field teams reported faster answer discovery, reducing escalation dependency</li>
  <li>Leadership and peer recognition described the new experience as "game changing"</li>
</ul>

<h3>Business Impact</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Reduced annual platform cost from $26,064 to $5,592 (~80% reduction)</li>
  <li>Year-one savings of $17,672 after accounting for migration effort</li>
  <li>Enabled modular, reusable content architecture for future scalability</li>
  <li>Zero documentation gaps during release cycles throughout the transition</li>
</ul>

<h2>Lessons Learned</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Parallel release coverage is non-negotiable during a migration; content freezes are rarely possible in enterprise products</li>
  <li>Two months of pre-production validation was critical; issues that appear minor in staging (broken cross-links, rendering differences) become customer-facing problems at scale</li>
  <li>Cost savings alone do not sell a migration internally; the improved customer experience (search, AI, modularity) is what gets leadership support</li>
</ul>

</CaseStudyLayout>
